    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d203">

                <pb facs="0407" n="373" sameAs="frus:frus1952-54v15p1/pg373"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">795.00/7–352: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Soviet Union (Kennan) to the Department of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">top secret</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1952-07-03T20:00:00+03:00">July 3, 1952—8 p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>27. Appreciate receiving inf contained Deptel
                    936, June 30<anchor n="1" xml:id="d203-f1"/> concerning Zinchenko talks. I am inclined, however, to
                    question whether we wld not be better advised to keep both channels, i.e.
                    Indian-Chi channel and Zinchenko channel,
                    open and active at this stage. This wld give us opportunity to detect any
                    differences of approach between Russ and Chi and a cross-check on any errors of
                    understanding or memory that <pb facs="0408" n="374" xml:id="pg374"/> may slip
                    into the somewhat involved and indirect channel to Chi Commies.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >Kennan</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d203-f1" n="1" xml:id="d203-f1-content">
                <p>In this telegram, the Department of State informed Kennan of the Zinchenko–Gross
                    conversation and stated that it did not intend to pursue this channel for fear
                    of “crossing wires” with the Indian-Chinese connection (795.00/6–3052).
                    Regarding this conversation, see telegram 986, <ref target="#pg364">p.
                    364</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
